# 0. An AI-oriented language

It's an interesting BOTH quest AND question: what an
AI-oriented programming language could look like? What properties it should
have? And where one goes with it when it comes to mixing knowledge, natural
language, with both structured and unstructured data. As well as evidence of
what is true and false, and how to reason about things in an almost precise
logical way.

More programs are written by a language model every year. A person states
what they want, an agent writes the program and runs it, and the person reads
the result. An **AI-oriented language** is a language designed for this
division of work: a model writes the program, a machine validates its types,
and a person can still read it.

A model makes mistakes of its own kind. It misspells a field, calls a function
that does not exist, or edits a program until a test passes. It also corrects
a mistake well when a report says exactly what is wrong. A language for this
writer rejects as many wrong programs as it can, and reports each rejection
precisely.

HQL is built on three observations:

1. Agents write SQL well. A language for them queries and transforms
   structured data the way SQL does, and reads that data from the systems
   where it lives.
2. Agents need type validation. Static types, contracts on data and errors kept as
   values turn a mistake into a report the agent can act on.
3. Markdown is the format people and models already exchange. A document that
   holds text, queries and their results is a place where both can work.

SQL is the language of structured data, and Markdown is the language of
written knowledge. HQL joins the two: a typed query language whose programs
also live inside Markdown documents.

```d2
direction: down

person: "person\nstates the task, reads the result" {
  shape: person
}
agent: "agent\nwrites the program" {
  shape: page
}
check: "type validation\nevery type in the program" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}
run: "run\nvalidate data against its contract" {
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}
values: "values\nresults and violations, both data" {
  shape: page
  style.fill: "#eef6ee"
}

person -> agent
agent -> check
check -> run: "the program fits its types"
run -> values
values -> person
check -> agent: "check error" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
  style.stroke-dash: 5
}
values -> agent: "violations" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
  style.stroke-dash: 5
}
```

Nothing here requires an agent. A person who writes HQL by hand gets the same
type validation and the same reports. The rest of this chapter shows each observation
as a small program, and names the chapter that covers it.

| Property | What the writer gets | Covered in |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Query steps like SQL's | a familiar way to ask questions of data | [SQL-like Queries](06-sql-like-queries.hmd) |
| Static types | a misspelt field or a missing function is a check error | [The type system](10-the-type-system/index.hmd) |
| Contracts on data | bad data is rejected where it is read | [Advanced data typing](11-advanced-data-typing/index.hmd) |
| Errors as values | a failure can be queried and routed | [Errors](12-errors/index.hmd) |
| Declarations with a Rust body | a function declared in HQL and implemented in your own Rust package | [Functions and declarations](32-functions-and-declarations/index.hmd) |
| Queries inside documents | text, program and result in one file | [Queries inside documents](24-documents.hmd) |

## Queries

Models have read a great deal of SQL, and they write it well. HQL asks the
same questions as SQL. Each question is a step in a pipeline:

```hql
shelf = [
    {title: "Frankenstein", genre: "novel", pages: 280},
    {title: "Dune", genre: "novel", pages: 412},
    {title: "Leaves of Grass", genre: "poetry", pages: 145},
]
shelf
| filter(b => b.genre == "novel")
| map(b => b.title)
```

```text
["Frankenstein", "Dune"]
```

`filter` is `WHERE` and `map` is `SELECT`. A step takes one value and returns
another, so a program is read from top to bottom, and a step can be added or
removed without touching the others.

Data for a question is seldom in one place. Orders are in a table, corrections
arrive on a topic, and the reason for an exception is in a note. HQL reads
files, tables, topics and Markdown documents with the same steps, so an agent
writes one program where it would otherwise write a query, a script and the
code that joins their answers.
[[book#part-ii-data-flows|Part II]] covers these systems.

## A wrong program is rejected

HQL is statically and strongly typed. **Type validation** compares every
expression of a program with the types it uses. It covers the whole program,
and `hql` runs only a program that passes. Here a field name is misspelt:

```hql
type Book = {title: String, pages: Int}

shelf = [
    Book(title = "Frankenstein", pages = 280),
    Book(title = "Dune", pages = 412),
]
shelf | map(b => b.page) | sum
```

```text
check error[unknown-field]: Book has no field `page`
 --> <expression>:7:18
  |
7 | shelf | map(b => b.page) | sum
  |                  ^^^^^^
```

Nothing ran. The report names the type, the field and the position. In a
language without type validation the program would run and return a missing value
or a wrong total, and the agent would have to find the cause from the result.
With the report, the correction is one word:

```hql
type Book = {title: String, pages: Int}

shelf = [
    Book(title = "Frankenstein", pages = 280),
    Book(title = "Dune", pages = 412),
]
shelf | map(b => b.pages) | sum
```

```text
692
```

The same holds for a function that is not there. `median` is declared in the
module `stats`, and this program does not import it:

```hql
[280, 412, 145] | median
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `median` is declared in the module `stats`: write `from stats import median`
 --> <expression>:1:19
  |
1 | [280, 412, 145] | median
  |                   ^^^^^^
```

The report states the line to write. HQL has no implicit conversions and no
undefined behaviour: a value is never converted without a written step, a
`match` must cover every case, and a division by zero stops the run with a
report. [[10-the-type-system|Chapter 10]] covers the type system.

## Bad data is a value

Type validation reads the program. It cannot read data that arrives while
the program runs. A **contract** covers that side. It is a type that states the
fields of a record, their types and the rules their values follow:

```hql
type BookData : DataContract = {
    title  : String
    pages  : Int
    status : String

    require pages > 0 as has_pages
    require ["todo", "reading", "done"] | contains(status) as known_status
}

shelf : List<Data> = [
    {title: "Dune", pages: 412, status: "reading"},
    {title: "The Hobbit", pages: 0, status: "lost"},
]

shelf
| validate(BookData)
| fork {
    clean    = oks()  | map(b => b.title),
    rejected = errs() | map(e => {title: e.value.title, rules: e.violations | map(v => v.rule)}),
}
```

```text
{clean: ["Dune"], rejected: [{rules: ["BookData.has_pages", "BookData.known_status"], title: "The Hobbit"}]}
```

`validate` checks each record against the contract and returns a result for
each: the typed record, or the record with its violations. It reports every
rule a record breaks, not only the first. `fork` sends the results to two
named branches. `oks()` keeps the accepted records and `errs()` keeps the
rejected ones.

The rejected side is a list of records like any other. A program can count
it, group it by rule, or write it to a table. An agent gets the failure as
data it can query, where an exception would give it a message to read.
[[11-advanced-data-typing|Chapter 11]] covers contracts and validation, and
[[12-errors|chapter 12]] covers errors.

## Declared in HQL, implemented in Rust

You can declare a function in HQL and write its body in Rust. The
declaration is the header only: a name, the parameters and the result type.
This one is from the module `hello`:

<!-- run: skip -->
```hql
/// The text greeting a name: `greet("Ada")` is `Hello, Ada!`.
fn greet(name: String) : String
```

The line has no `=` and no body. The body is a Rust function in a package
that you write:

```rust
/// `fn greet(name: String) : String`
fn greet(
    _cx: &mut dyn EvalCx,
    input: Value,
    _arguments: &[TypedArg],
    span: Span,
) -> Result<Value, Diagnostic> {
    Ok(Value::Str(hello(&text(&input, &span)?, "!").into()))
}
```

A **package** is a Rust crate with two parts: the HQL module files that hold
the declarations, and the Rust library that implements them. A program
imports the module and calls the function:

<!-- run: --project examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello

hello.greet("Ada")
```

```text
"Hello, Ada!"
```

The declaration says that `greet` takes a `String` and returns a `String`.
That is all type validation needs for a call. It does not read the Rust
body. `hello.greet("Ada")` is accepted, and `hello.greet(1)` is a check error.

This gives a program two levels. HQL is the high level: short, typed
pipelines over data. Rust is the low level: anything a Rust crate can do,
from a numeric library to a client for another system. The declaration is the
boundary between them, and it is a typed one. An agent can write both sides.
Rust is statically typed, handles errors as values, and has no undefined
behaviour in safe code, so the guarantees this chapter describes for HQL hold
below the boundary as well.
[[book#part-v-extending-hql|Part V]] covers declarations, extensions and
packages, and [[34-writing-an-extension|chapter 34]] writes the `hello`
package from an empty directory.

## Markdown as a computational medium

Queries, types and contracts cover one side of an agent's work: SQL, big
data, OLAP and structured data processing. The other side is knowledge
written in natural language.

Markdown has effectively become a common medium for exchanging knowledge
between humans and AI agents. What if we could combine that with the
computational capabilities of the language itself?

So basically, how far can one push the mix of natural and formal language? No
idea, but maybe we can evolve or push Markdown a bit more. Stay with me on
this one.

Think of how useful Jupyter notebooks have been for data science in Python.
I've been experimenting with something similar for Markdown, where fenced HQL
expressions become executable cells. The results of their evaluation are
written back into the document, alongside the queries.

The [HyperMarkDown](https://hypermarkdown.org) project calls these *HQL cells*.
The HQL binary is shipped with an existing [VS Code extension](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=hypermarkdown.hmd), so you can try them directly in
the editor. An ordinary Markdown document becomes a computational notebook with wiki-links and structured metadata.

## Human in the loop and recursive evaluation

This is perhaps the most interesting and the most speculative direction. Once
Markdown documents contain executable cells and can reference and transclude
other documents, we no longer need to think of an agent's context as a single
static prompt, but rather as many versions of its parts.

This is data composition together with dynamic REPL behavior. Instead of a
static prompt, we can construct a tree or graph of Markdown cards, each
containing natural language knowledge, queries, intermediate results, or even
instructions for another agent. A larger prompt can emerge from the
evaluation of these interconnected cards.

An agent can modify a query, generate a new card, or update an existing one.
The resulting changes can then propagate through a cascade of dependent
evaluations. A human can inspect any individual card, modify its instructions
or data, and re-evaluate a particular part of the graph.

This opens up interesting questions about dependency management, versioning,
stale results, context drift and recursive evaluation. Unlike a conventional
chat session, the evolving context is explicit, inspectable and versionable.

The next milestone is to make agents themselves definable as HyperMarkDown
cards. The broader experiment is whether we can bring together natural
language, strongly typed computation and agentic reasoning in a way that
preserves meaningful human control over the entire process.


